  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Family: Ariidae. Size: 55 cm total length. Climate: subtropical, 41°N – 28°S. IUCN Red List: Not Evaluated.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: widely distributed in southern Africa; in freshwater, estuaries and coastal environments. Type locality: around Cape Town [South Africa].</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Generally found in large shoals on muddy bottoms in turbid waters, usually on the coastline and in estuaries. Also found in rivers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on crayfish, small fish and crabs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Dimorphism of the posterior (humeral) process of the cleithrum is obvious externally: fan-shaped in females, triangular in males. The maxillary barbel is longer in females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/galeichthys_feliceps.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: vertebrae 51–52 (17 precaudal, 34–35 caudal). Head longer than, or nearly as long as, broad; relatively long and thin maxillary barbel, extending at least to the pectoral fin spine origin; deeply forked caudal fin with pointed lobes, the upper lobe longer; 11–14 gill rakers on the anterior face of the first arch; 10–12 pectoral fin rays; upper two-thirds of the body darkened, with some lateral speckling and a stark white belly.</p><p>Etymology: the specific name feliceps comes from felis, cat, and ceps, head, i.e. \"cathead\", a variation on the term \"catfish\", referring to the cat-like whiskers (barbels).</p><p>Remarks: considered a nuisance by shore and ski-boat anglers in southern Africa as little else is caught. The spines are poisonous and wounds should be treated immediately.</p>"
    }
  ],
